## Proof

Let $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ be closed, with $S \subseteq \{(x,y) : a < x < b\}$. Let
$$\pi_y(S) = \{y \in \mathbb{R} : \exists\, x \in \mathbb{R},\; (x,y) \in S\}$$
be the projection of $S$ onto the $y$-axis. We show $\pi_y(S)$ is closed by showing it contains all its limit points.

Let $(y_n)$ be a sequence in $\pi_y(S)$ with $y_n \to y_0 \in \mathbb{R}$. We must show $y_0 \in \pi_y(S)$.

Since each $y_n \in \pi_y(S)$, there exists $x_n$ such that $(x_n, y_n) \in S$. By hypothesis $S \subseteq \{a < x < b\}$, so $x_n \in (a, b) \subseteq [a, b]$ for every $n$.

The closed interval $[a, b]$ is compact (Heine–Borel), so the sequence $(x_n)$, being bounded in $[a,b]$, admits a convergent subsequence $(x_{n_k})$ with
$$x_{n_k} \to x_0 \in [a, b].$$

Since $y_{n_k} \to y_0$ (as a subsequence of a convergent sequence), we have
$$(x_{n_k},\, y_{n_k}) \to (x_0,\, y_0) \quad \text{in } \mathbb{R}^2.$$

Each $(x_{n_k}, y_{n_k}) \in S$, and $S$ is closed, hence $S$ contains all its limit points. Therefore
$$(x_0, y_0) \in S.$$

Because $S \subseteq \{a < x < b\}$, the point $(x_0, y_0) \in S$ satisfies $a < x_0 < b$. In particular, $x_0$ is a valid $x$-coordinate witnessing membership, so
$$y_0 \in \pi_y(S).$$

Thus every convergent sequence in $\pi_y(S)$ has its limit in $\pi_y(S)$, which (in $\mathbb{R}$, sequential closedness equals closedness) means $\pi_y(S)$ is closed. $\blacksquare$

**Key idea**: The hypothesis $S \subseteq \{a < x < b\}$ confines the $x$-coordinates of $S$ to a bounded interval. This boundedness provides the compactness needed to extract a convergent subsequence of the $x_n$'s; closedness of $S$ then forces the limit point $(x_0, y_0)$ back into $S$, and the strip condition guarantees $x_0$ is a legitimate witness so that $y_0$ lands in the projection. Without the boundedness in $x$ (e.g. if $S$ were only known to lie in a half-plane), the argument would fail — and indeed the projection of a closed set need not be closed in general (e.g. $\{(x, 1/x) : x \ge 1\}$ is closed but its projection $(0,1]$ is not).

